American Energy Market Regulator (AEMR) SQL Case Study
Project Overview

This project analyzes network reliability and energy loss patterns for the American Energy Market Regulator (AEMR), which oversees the U.S. domestic energy network. The study examines outage data (planned and unplanned) from multiple providers during 2016–2017, aiming to identify reliability risks and performance drivers through SQL analysis and Tableau storytelling.

The case addresses two key management priorities:

Energy Stability & Market Outages – Frequency and causes of outages

Energy Losses & Market Reliability – Impact of outages on total energy loss and system resilience

Objectives

Determine which outage types are most frequent and disruptive

Identify market participants responsible for the highest energy losses

Analyze year-over-year trends to highlight reliability changes

Provide data-driven recommendations for improving network stability

Dataset Description

The dataset covers outages submitted by multiple U.S. energy providers and includes:

Outage Type: Consequential, Forced, Opportunistic, Planned

Outage Status: Approved or Canceled

Outage Duration: Start and end timestamps

Energy Lost (MW): Quantitative measure of impact

Market Participant Information: Provider name and ID

Tools & Technologies

SQL: Data extraction, cleaning, and aggregation (via Jupyter Notebook using %%sql magic)

Python (Jupyter): Query execution and validation

Tableau: Visualization and executive storytelling

PowerPoint / Google Slides: Final management presentation

Analysis Process
